==Middle English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|enm|wæld}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|enm|ang|weald||high land covered with wood, woods, forest}}, from {{inh|enm|gem-pro|*walþuz}}, whence also Old High German {{m|goh|wald}} (German {{m|de|Wald}}) and Old Norse {{m|non|vǫllr}} (Faroese {{m|fo|vøllur}}, Norwegian {{m|no|voll}}, Icelandic {{m|is|völlur}}).

===Noun===
{{enm-noun|pl1=waldis|pl2=walden}}

# a wooded area, forested land, the woods; a wooded tract, forest preserve; the forest as a wild place
#: ''Þe wurmes & te wilde deor ... o þis '''wald''' wunieð.'' &mdash; St. Margaret of Antioch, c1225
#: ''Ȝif æi mon hine mihte ifinden uppe þissere '''wælden''','' ... &mdash; Layamon's Brut, c1275
#: ''Beliagog in þat nede Fond him riche '''wald''' To fine.'' &mdash; Sir Tristrem, c1330
#: ''Was nouthire '''waldis''' in þar walke ne watir to fynde.'' &mdash; Wars of Alexander, 1450

